  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to find a sparse representation of the step function that minimizes overlapping integrals. Unlike continuous solution approaches, this method discretizes the function using integer-spaced window centers and pulse durations. The rationale is that integer-spaced representations can exactly match the discrete grid used in the objective evaluation, while still potentially achieving lower overlap. This strategy combines deterministic search over limited parameter spaces with transformations to generate scaled solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe optimization targets an integer n (approx 100) representing samples. The function h(x) must satisfy:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   - Sum(h) = n/2 (due to discrete integral)\nThe overlap integral is computed using np.correlate with window sizes and offsets. The search concentrates on configurations with at most 1 spike, then missing fewer elements, leveraging the observation that most optimal solutions exhibit minimal structure.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep 1: Scale the parent solution to target n\nStep 2: Generate candidates by mutating window center and height\nStep 3: Fitness evaluated via exact/full correlation computation\nStep 4: Select most promising child and gradually add structure\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Primary variation: Lengthened parent with sporadic spikes\n- Secondary variation: Regular spacing with fewer spikes\n- Tertiary variation: Coarser grid with limited structure\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n- Time dominated by correlation calculations\n- Process samples incrementally to maintain feasibility\n- Fall back to parent if no improvement in 100 attempts\n\n6. Validation and contract\n- Return vectors satisfying integral constraint (within tolerance)\n- Re-check overlap score via specified method\n- Ensure n is greater than 200 for effective structure",
